module bindings.python.redirectFollowCredentialHeaders;

// The Python twin of bindings.javascript.redirectFollowCredentialHeaders: a
// client that follows a 3xx itself decides what the next request carries, and
// the part that cannot simply be copied is the credential set -- `Authorization`
// and `Cookie` first among them -- because the 3xx is written by the server the
// first request reached and names the next host, so a copy of the header set
// hands that server's operator whatever bearer token, session cookie or
// basic-auth credential the caller attached for the original host. That is
// CWE-200 with no injection anywhere in it: the URL the client fetches is
// exactly the one it was told to fetch.
//
// The fact recorded here is the same one the JavaScript binding records, read
// through the tokens the Python frontend emits for a function context: a
// `Location` value read, which is what makes the code a redirect *follower*
// rather than a redirect *emitter*; a header set of the next request being
// subscripted (`.headers[`, the deletion or read every hop builder performs on
// the copied header store); and a redirect budget stored on the request object
// -- `.max_redirects=`, `.follow_redirects=`, `.redirect_count=` -- which is
// what separates a client building the *next* hop from a caller or a test
// merely reading a 3xx response: RFC 7231 requires a follower to bound the
// chain, so every implementation carries such a field and nothing else does.
// The budget needles carry their leading dot because a Python assignment token
// embeds the whole right-hand side text, and without it
// `response = self.fetch(url, follow_redirects=False)` -- the ordinary shape of
// a test asserting on a 3xx -- matches the keyword argument inside the call.
//
// The negation names what a follower that handles the hop boundary has to
// spell: a credential header name in an assignment (a strip list held in a
// local, or a rebuilt header store), or the credential fields of the request
// object being cleared (`auth_username=`, `auth_password=`, `auth=`), which is
// load-bearing rather than incidental wherever the library materializes those
// fields into the Authorization header on each connection. It deliberately
// does not read the `literal` tokens: on a Python function context a `literal`
// predicate routes through the file's constant nodes with no function anchor,
// so a credential header spelled anywhere in the module -- including in the
// constructor that *builds* the Authorization header the leak is about --
// would silence the follower that never considers dropping it. The comparison
// against the redirect target's origin is not named either: `scheme`, `netloc`
// and `host` are read for a dozen unrelated reasons, and the helper that
// performs the comparison is named freely.
//
// Residual, in the over-reporting direction, both from the same cause: a strip
// written as a bare list literal in a `for` statement names the headers only
// in `literal` tokens -- which this binding cannot read without inheriting the
// file-wide silencing above -- so it is reported unless the credential fields
// are cleared in the same function; and a strip list held in a module-level
// constant is outside the function context altogether. The false-negative
// direction is comment-proof: assignment and literal tokens are AST-derived,
// and prose naming Authorization above the copy does not stop the report.
binding pyRedirectFollowKeepsCredentialHeaders {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and containsAny(node.context.literal, ["Location", "location"])
    and containsAny(node.context.subscript, [".headers["])
    and containsAny(node.context.assign, [".max_redirects=", ".follow_redirects=", ".redirect_count="])
    and not containsAny(node.context.assign, ["Authorization", "Cookie", "Cookie2", "www-authenticate", "WWW-Authenticate", "Www-Authenticate", "proxy-authorization", "Proxy-Authorization", "auth_username=", "auth_password=", "auth="])
  emit issue code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect at node
}

// The sibling of pyRedirectFollowKeepsCredentialHeaders for the client whose
// redirect budget is not a field on the request object but a Retry object,
// re-entered through a recursive call to the request API: urllib3's
// PoolManager.urlopen and every copy of it vendored into a downstream
// distribution. The same three family facts in that library's spelling. The
// Location read is delegated to the documented response accessor
// `get_redirect_location`, so the follower's own body carries no `Location`
// literal and the literal route above cannot see it. The header store is a
// kwargs subscript -- `kw['headers']` -- the dict the recursive hop receives
// unchanged, which is the copy that carries the credential headers to the
// host the 3xx names. And the next hop is the recursive `urlopen` call, the
// library's documented request method: what separates a client building the
// next request from a caller or a test merely reading a 3xx is that the
// follower calls the request API again, and in this family the RFC 7231 chain
// bound lives on the Retry object handed to that call.
//
// The control the library spells inside the follower is the option read
// `retries.remove_headers_on_redirect` -- the documented public API for this
// strip (`Retry`'s `remove_headers_on_redirect` parameter, defaulted to the
// Authorization header and carried unchanged from 1.23 through 2.x, where only
// the origin check beside it was renamed). The header name itself lives in
// the Retry class's DEFAULT_REDIRECT_HEADERS_BLACKLIST constant in another
// module, so the name is deliberately not a needle here: a follower
// consulting the option through the attribute read is handling the boundary
// whatever the list holds. The credential-name negation on assignment tokens
// is the family's, unchanged, and covers an independently spelled strip list
// held in a local. The origin comparison (`is_same_host`, `is_same_origin` in
// later revisions) is not a needle, for the family's reason: a follower that
// compares origins but strips nothing would be silenced by it.
//
// Residual, false-negative direction: the connection-level and app-engine
// followers in the same package (HTTPConnectionPool.urlopen,
// AppEngineManager.urlopen) forward their `headers` parameter positionally to
// the recursive hop, and a positional argument produces no token the Python
// frontend emits, so the header-store fact cannot see them; so does a follower
// whose request method is not named urlopen. False-positive direction inherits
// the family's: a strip list held in a module-level constant, or spelled only
// as the bare iterable of a for statement, is invisible to the assignment
// route and is reported.
binding pyRetryBoundedRedirectFollowKeepsCredentialHeaders {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and containsAny(node.context.call, ["get_redirect_location"])
    and containsAny(node.context.subscript, ["['headers']", ".headers["])
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["urlopen"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.selector, ["remove_headers_on_redirect"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.assign, ["Authorization", "Cookie", "Cookie2", "www-authenticate", "WWW-Authenticate", "Www-Authenticate", "proxy-authorization", "Proxy-Authorization", "auth_username=", "auth_password=", "auth="])
  emit issue code.CredentialHeadersForwardedAcrossRedirect at node
}
